Help-desk and customer-support teams receive the same problem described in many different words, through app reviews, support tickets, chat and web forms. Agents triage each report separately, and nobody sees how many customers one problem affects. Existing tools group complaints by theme rather than by problem, and flag spikes with statistics that fail at small counts. Counting, alerting and escalation all depend on one step that these tools skip: deciding, as each complaint arrives, whether it reports a problem already known or a new one.

This project presents InsightDesk, an online pipeline that groups raw customer complaints into problem-level cases and turns those cases into incident signals. Each complaint is cleaned, embedded with a MiniLM sentence encoder and compared with up to five diverse exemplars of every existing case stored in a Pinecone vector index. Two thresholds calibrated from labelled pairs settle the clear matches and clear non-matches; only the uncertain complaints in between are sent to an open-weight large language model (gpt-oss-120b) that makes one bounded decision: join a candidate case, open a new case, or mark the complaint as too vague to place. A re-joining pass every six hours merges cases that the online decisions split. A Poisson CUSUM detects spikes per case and per group of related cases in six-hour windows, and a separate alarm watches complaints too vague to join any case. Cases receive sentiment, a topic and a department; an eligible case gets an evidence-grounded root-cause explanation, emailed to the department only after an operator approves it. The system is built with FastAPI, Supabase, Pinecone, Groq and React.

The pipeline was evaluated on 338 human-labelled Google Play reviews of six help-desk products, with thresholds always tuned on one half of the problems and scored on the other. It reaches pairwise precision 0.83, recall 0.35 and F1 0.49, against F1 of at most 0.25 for published grouping methods and topic models run on the same data. With the same upper threshold it reaches precision 0.87–0.96 on Mozilla Bugzilla duplicate benchmarks (F1 0.78–0.79), case precision 0.92 on three unseen products and 0.89 on an unseen company. On outbreaks injected into real complaint streams, the CUSUM detects 76–91% of outbreaks on bug-report streams at under one false alarm per 100 windows, where the z-score rule of feedback tools detects none, and every outage made only of vague complaints is detected once it reaches ten complaints. These results show that a calibrated similarity band with a bounded LLM judge, and small-count statistics, make complaint grouping and incident detection work at the volumes support desks see.

*Keywords:* complaint triage, duplicate detection, LLM-as-a-judge, sentence embeddings, online clustering, spike detection, Poisson CUSUM, sentiment analysis, topic modelling, root-cause analysis, human-in-the-loop
